= 1
{parent=7}

= a
{parent=Paper 61/7/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $r=x-u^*$. If $u^*$ is a <best approximation> in the finite-dimensional <linear subspace>, then for every $v$ in it and every real $t$,
$$
0\le\|r-tv\|^2-\|r\|^2=-2t\operatorname{Re}(r,v)+t^2\|v\|^2.
$$
A nonzero real linear <coefficient> would make this negative for sufficiently small $t$ of the appropriate sign. Hence $\operatorname{Re}(r,v)=0$. In a complex <inner product space>, apply the argument to $iv$ too; the imaginary part also vanishes. Thus $(x-u^*,v)=0$ for every $v$ in the subspace.

Conversely, if that orthogonality holds, every other candidate has the form $u^*+v$, and the <Pythagorean theorem> in the <inner product> norm gives
$$
\|x-u^*-v\|^2=\|x-u^*\|^2+\|v\|^2\ge\|x-u^*\|^2.
$$
Equality requires $v=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{u^*\text{ is the unique best approximant}\ \Longleftrightarrow\ x-u^*\perp\mathcal U_n.}
$$
The next part constructs this <orthogonal projection> explicitly. No completeness of the whole <inner product space> is required for projection onto a finite-dimensional subspace.

= b
{parent=Paper 61/7/1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take the <inner product> linear in its first argument; for a real space no conjugation convention is needed. Insert $u^*=\sum_{j=1}^n a_j u_j$ into the orthogonality condition against each <basis> vector $u_i$. The <normal equations> are
$$
\boxed{\sum_{j=1}^n(u_j,u_i)a_j=(x,u_i),\qquad1\le i\le n.}
$$
Equivalently $Ha=b$, where $H_{ij}=(u_j,u_i)$ and $b_i=(x,u_i)$. This orientation of the <Gram matrix> matches the stated inner-product convention. It is Hermitian and positive definite: for a nonzero <coefficient> vector $a$,
$$
a^*Ha=\left\|\sum_j a_j u_j\right\|^2>0
$$
by <linear independence> of the <basis>. Hence $H$ is invertible and $a=H^{-1}b$ gives existence and uniqueness of $u^*$. For an <orthonormal basis> this simplifies to $a_i=(x,u_i)$; for a general <basis> the Gram inverse correctly accounts for interactions between <basis> vectors.

= 2
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Use the standard $L_\infty$-oriented partition normalization of <B-splines>, in contrast to the unit-integral normalization. Thus $N_i\ge0$ and $\sum_iN_i(t)\le1$ on $[0,1]$, with equality for a complete <basis> on its basic knot interval. This terminology does not mean that each <basis> function's maximum is exactly one; for example an order-three cardinal spline has maximum $3/4$. Assume the <basis> has positive support on the integration interval and is continuous, as is needed for a projection taking values in $C[0,1]$.

Let
$$
H_{ij}=\int_0^1N_i(t)N_j(t)\,dt,\qquad d_i=\int_0^1N_i(t)\,dt>0,\qquad D=\operatorname{diag}(d_i).
$$
The ordinary <Gram matrix> $H$ is positive definite by <linear independence>. The appropriate row-normalized, or <mixed-normalization spline Gram matrix>, is
$$
\boxed{G=D^{-1}H,\qquad G_{ij}=\int_0^1M_i(t)N_j(t)\,dt,\qquad M_i=N_i/d_i.}
$$
Here $M_i$ is a unit-integral nonnegative spline. Unlike $H$, $G$ need not be symmetric. If the full support lies inside the interval, $d_i=(t_{i+k}-t_i)/k$; otherwise use the actual interval integral rather than that full-support formula.

The <normal equations> for the $L_2$ <orthogonal projection> $P_{\mathcal S}f=\sum_i a_iN_i$ say $Ha=c$, with $c_i=\int fN_i$. Divide each row by $d_i$ to obtain
$$
Ga=b,\qquad b_i=\int_0^1f(t)M_i(t)\,dt.
$$
Nonnegativity and unit integral give $|b_i|\le\|f\|_\infty$. The <subpartition of unity for B-splines> then gives
$$
|P_{\mathcal S}f(t)|\le\sum_i|a_i|N_i(t)\le\|a\|_{\ell^\infty}\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}\|f\|_\infty.
$$
Taking the supremum over $t$ and then over nonzero $f$ proves the <uniform norm bound for B-spline orthogonal projection>:
$$
\boxed{\|P_{\mathcal S}\|_{C[0,1]\to C[0,1]}\le\|G^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}.}
$$
The row normalization is essential; replacing $G$ with the ordinary <Gram matrix> would not be the same asserted estimate. The operator is defined on $C[0,1]$ and maps into its finite-dimensional spline subspace, **onto $\mathcal S$, not onto all of $C[0,1]$** as one phrase in the PDF says. Its range follows both from the formula and from reproducing every $s\in\mathcal S$.

If “$L_\infty$-normalized” were instead read literally as individually peak-one splines, their sum can exceed one. The basis-dependent factor $K=\|\sum_iN_i\|_\infty$ must then be retained: $\|P_{\mathcal S}\|_\infty\le K\|(D^{-1}H)^{-1}\|_{\ell^\infty}$. Equivalently the displayed bound without an outside factor uses $G=K^{-1}D^{-1}H$. The standard partition-normalized convention has $K\le1$ and yields the sharper bound proved above.
